      "lineNbr": 204,
      "author": {
        "id": 1693933
      },
      "writtenOn": "2023-08-29T08:49:28Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The ATV documentation suggests LEANBACK for checking a TV device: https://developer.android.com/training/tv/start/hardware.html#runtime-check. And it seems `FEATURE_TELEVISION` is deprecated by `FEATURE_LEANBACK`: https://cs.android.com/android/platform/superproject/main/+/main:frameworks/base/core/java/android/content/pm/PackageManager.java?q\u003dsymbol%3A%5Cbandroid.content.pm.PackageManager.FEATURE_TELEVISION%5Cb%20case%3Ayes\n\n------\n\n\u003e Or, is there a way to know if the device is always connected to power ?\nI don\u0027t think we should check by whether it\u0027s connected to power, because this initialization code is run when the system_server boots and the feature availability shouldn\u0027t be detected by a runtime factor which can change at any time.\n\n\n\nI am hesitated adding the TV and SDK level checks. I think the FEATURE_THREAD_NETWORK availability check is good enough since there are no existing devices have the Thread feature.\n\n1. Removing the TV check allows Google and partners to enable Thread on U devices (such as tablets) if they backport the Thread HAL service.\n2. Removing the SDK level check allows partners to enable Thread on older platforms. Partners did reach to our partner engineers to ask if we can support older platforms. @rochaferraz@google.com\n\n\n@lorenzo@google.com Thoughts?",
      "parentUuid": "25785386_e5251f7f",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 204,
        "startChar": 78,
        "endLine": 204,
        "endChar": 94
      },
